The code that follows this guideline is easier to maintain, adapt, and refactor, has less coupling between methods, less duplication,
and better information hiding. On the other hand, you may need to write many wrapper methods to meet this guideline.

**Example:**


      boolean pay(Customer c, Invoice invoice) {
        int dollars = c.getWallet().contents; // violation
        if (dollars >= invoice.getAmount()) {
          Wallet w = c.getWallet();
          w.subtract(invoice.getAmount()); // violation
          return true;
        }
        return false;
      }

The above example might be better implemented as a method `payInvoice(Invoice invoice)` in `Customer`.


Use the **Ignore calls to library methods and access to library fields** option to ignore Law of Demeter violations
that can't be fixed without changing a library.

Inspection ID: LawOfDemeter

fullDescription.text:
